        // a second call: if rewardsForA[recipient] == 0 then calling getFirstWithdrawalBonus(recipient)
        // a second time will still succeed (it will add 100 and withdraw 100 again). However, if rewardsForA[recipient]
        // remained nonzero from the first call in the mutant, the second call's behavior could differ only
        // in that the prior balance would have been nonzero — we cannot read private state directly.
        // Instead we use the observable rule: after the first call, attempting to withdraw the recipient's reward
        // again (by calling getFirstWithdrawalBonus(recipient)) should still succeed on the reference contract.
        // If the mutant left rewardsForA[recipient] nonzero because funds were mis-sent to msg.sender, the second
        // invocation may still behave similarly; to directly test the private storage being zero we exploit the
        // fact that the reference's withdrawReward sets rewardsForA[recipient] to 0 regardless of external success.
        // Therefore the reliable observable: calling getFirstWithdrawalBonus(recipient) twice in a row from the same caller
        // on the reference will both succeed. The mutant can cause the first to succeed but the second to revert
        // if the low-level call target differs and reverts — however, to keep the property universal and directly checking
        // contract's behavior we instead assert that after the first call the contract's balance decreased by 100 wei
        // (the bonus amount) if and only if the intended recipient received the funds. The reference always sends to recipient,
        // so the contract's balance always decreases by exactly 100. The mutant sends to msg.sender and thus when caller != recipient
        // the contract's balance still decreases by 100 (but to a different external), however the only reliable universal observation
        // that differs is: when caller != recipient and the callee (recipient) is a contract that accepts funds while caller may revert on receive,
        // the mutant can cause different revert behavior. To keep this test strictly black-box and universal, we instead assert that
